46. GROUP BY
GROUP BY is used to group rows with similar values and perform aggregate calculations on each group

Example Table
Name	Dept	Salary
Ravi	IT	  50000
John	IT	  60000
Sam	  HR	  40000

SELECT department,
AVG(salary)
FROM employees
GROUP BY department;

Output:-
Department	Avg Salary
  IT	       55000
  HR	        40000


47. HAVING
HAVING filters grouped records after GROUP BY has been applied.

SELECT department,
AVG(salary)
FROM employees
GROUP BY department
HAVING AVG(salary) > 50000;

Output
Department
IT


48. Difference Between WHERE and HAVING
WHERE filters rows before grouping, while HAVING filters groups after aggregation.

      WHERE                                       	HAVING
     Filters rows	                               ilters groups
Used before GROUP BY	                       Used after GROUP BY
Cannot use aggregate functions directly	   Can use aggregate functions

WHERE Example - Filters individual rows.

SELECT *
FROM employees
WHERE salary > 50000;

HAVING Example - Filters groups.

SELECT department,
AVG(salary)
FROM employees
GROUP BY department
HAVING AVG(salary) > 50000;



49. Aggregate vs Scalar Functions
Aggregate functions - work on multiple rows and return a single result
scalar functions - operate on individual valuess

Aggregate Functions - Operate on multiple rows and return one value.
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Examples:
COUNT()
SUM()
AVG()
MIN()
MAX()

Example - Returns one value.

SELECT AVG(salary)
FROM employees;

Scalar Functions - Operate on one value and return one value.
-----------------------------------------------------------
Examples:
UPPER()
LOWER()
LENGTH()
ROUND()

SELECT UPPER(name)
FROM employees;

Output:
RAVI
JOHN
SAM


50. ROLLUP - CUBE
ROLLUP generates subtotals and grand totals
CUBE generates totals for all possible combinations of grouped columns

ROLLUP - Creates subtotals and grand totals.
-------------------------------------------
Example
SELECT department,
SUM(salary)
FROM employees
GROUP BY ROLLUP(department);

Output:
Department	Total Salary
IT	         110000
HR	         40000
NULL	       150000

NULL row = Grand Total

CUBE
Generates all possible combinations and totals.




























































